<p>In the latest development, U.S. naval forces enforcing a blockade around Iranian coastal areas and ports have prompted some ships to slow, stop, or reverse course in and near the Strait of Hormuz, one of the world’s most important oil chokepoints. U.S. Central Command said 10 vessels had turned back within 48 hours and that no ships sailing from Iran had broken through since the blockade began on Monday. At the same time, several reports said U.S.-sanctioned supertankers still entered the Gulf through the strait, underscoring the limits and legal complexity of a blockade focused on traffic to and from Iranian ports rather than all movement through international waters. The immediate effect is lighter shipping near Iran and fresh uncertainty around regional energy flows, even as some tanker traffic continues through the waterway.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/N4_-y9EgIJJANtPVAVwWBc0My7SFNrqm-kCh2bIn2PhmhaFUm_yqakHv2glWe9EyrPx46cfirDldIji1A5_35bIswryvbkOkydgGgVIX3XxwwCDDAfWO6VrYTNbjTBClJ7o7qso1yWJX4GtTF4ZQkUF4qjU0v--fa3KmQ_-MEBBBFPeGv2Zq-wwTbyzKJlrXjzolM_WOWYT5-9cCamB9ecy6lPlb1A' alt='A vessel in the Strait of Hormuz on Sunday. Traffic is still at a fraction of its usual levels.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Broad reach: The New York Times reported that the blockade applies to vessels from any country if they are entering or leaving Iranian coastal areas or ports.</li><li>Clear destination: The Hindu reported that one sanctioned supertanker crossed the strait heading for Iran’s Imam Khomeini port despite the blockade.</li><li>Tracking left on: Iran’s Fars News Agency said one tanker kept its tracking system on &quot;without any concealment&quot; during the voyage.</li><li>Large naval presence: More than a dozen U.S. Navy warships were reported to be enforcing the blockade on vessels entering or leaving Iranian coastal areas or ports.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>U.S. Central Command: CENTCOM cast the early response as a sign that the blockade was already deterring voyages tied to Iranian ports soon after it began. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/us-says-10-vessels-turned-back-in-48-hours-of-iran-port-blockade'>Straits Times</a>, <a href='https://www.nbcnews.com/world/iran/live-blog/live-updates-us-blockade-iran-hormuz-trump-peace-talks-rcna331890/rcrd108066?canonicalCard=true'>NBC News</a>)</li><li>Iran&#x27;s Fars News Agency: Fars described the tanker passage as lawful transit through international waters, arguing that the voyage did not violate a blockade centered on Iranian ports. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/iran-war-strait-of-hormuz-us-blockade-sanctioned-ships-tankers-transit/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>Commercial shippers: Route data suggested operators were distinguishing between ordinary Hormuz passage and voyages that would approach Iranian ports, adjusting itineraries based on destination risk. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/15/world/middleeast/iran-ships-strait-hormuz-blockade.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/15/world/middleeast/iran-ships-strait-hormuz-blockade.html'>Iran-Linked Ships Slow or Stop as U.S. Navy Enforces Strait of Hormuz Blockade</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/us-sanctioned-supertankers-enter-gulf-despite-blockade'>US-sanctioned supertankers enter Gulf despite blockade</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.reddit.com/r/worldnews/comments/1smr8f9/us_navy_blockade_seems_to_be_working_as/'>US Navy blockade seems to be working as Iran-linked vessels halt or reverse course in Strait of Hormuz</a> - reddit.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-iran-israel-war-2nd-us-sanctioned-supertanker-enters-gulf-despite-blockade-on-iranian-ports-middle-east-conflict-11364529#publisher=newsstand'>2nd US-Sanctioned Supertanker Enters Gulf Despite Blockade On Iranian Ports</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a 